smallest reproducible lab for lesson 3, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Fortinet</span><h3>4. BGP multipath before SD-WAN steering</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-configuring-sd-wan-traffic-steering-with-bgp-learned-routes-229549">https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-configuring-sd-wan-traffic-steering-with-bgp-learned-routes-229549</a></p><h4>What the Fortinet example proves</h4><p>Fortinet’s August 24, 2026 technical tip demonstrates a subtle but critical distinction: two eBGP sessions can both be Established and the BGP table can contain two valid paths while the routing table installs only one best path. SD-WAN cannot steer over a BGP path that the routing process has not installed as a usable next hop.</p><h4>Control-plane sequence</h4><p>The local FortiGate learns 192.168.2.0/24 over two eBGP peers. Before multipath, both paths are visible in BGP but only one becomes active in the RIB. Enabling `set ebgp-multipath enable` allows both otherwise-equal paths to be installed. SD-WAN then has two real forwarding candidates instead of merely two BGP advertisements.</p><h4>Verification chain</h4><p>The source deliberately verifies several state boundaries: `get router info bgp summary` confirms peer state; `get router info bgp network &lt;prefix&gt;` shows candidate BGP paths; the routing-table command proves which next hops are installed; `diagnose sys sdwan service` shows member eligibility; and session output proves the actual gateway/member used by a real flow. The last step matters because policy intent is not proof of forwarding.</p><h4>Failure diagnosis</h4><p>If the second peer is Established but only one next hop is active, changing SD-WAN priority is premature. Compare BGP attributes and multipath eligibility first. If both next hops are installed but only one SD-WAN member is eligible, move to SD-WAN service, health-check, and policy state.</p><h4>Interoperability</h4><p>The same principle applies when the upstream peers are Cisco, Arista, Juniper, or FRR. Control-plane diversity becomes useful to FortiGate SD-WAN only after FortiOS has selected and installed multiple forwarding paths.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>get router info bgp summary
get router info bgp network &lt;prefix&gt;
get router info routing-table details &lt;prefix&gt;
diagnose sys sdwan service
diagnose sys session list</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 4, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Fortinet</span><h3>5. Performance SLA when BGP remains Established</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-using-sd-wan-performance-sla-to-steer-traffic-when-bgp-remains-established-229544">https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-using-sd-wan-performance-sla-to-steer-traffic-when-bgp-remains-established-229544</a></p><h4>Why BGP Established is insufficient</h4><p>Fortinet’s companion August 24 article shows a failure beyond the BGP peer while the BGP TCP session and learned routes remain healthy. BGP proves reachability between the two BGP endpoints and continued route exchange; it does not prove that an application destination behind the peer remains reachable.</p><h4>SLA design</h4><p>Fortinet configures an SD-WAN Performance SLA toward a target inside the remote service path rather than merely pinging the BGP neighbor. Both SD-WAN members are monitored, with explicit source addresses so each probe tests the intended path. A valid return path to those source addresses is required.</p><h4>Failure sequence</h4><p>When reachability through one member is intentionally broken beyond the BGP peer, `diagnose sys sdwan health-check` marks that member dead while both BGP sessions remain Established and both BGP next hops remain in the routing table. `diagnose sys sdwan service` then removes the unhealthy member from selection without requiring BGP withdrawal.</p><h4>Packet proof</h4><p>Session inspection shows the selected gateway and fields such as `sdwan_mbr_seq` and `sdwan_service_id`. Those fields prove that an actual flow followed the surviving path. This is stronger evidence than simply reading the configured service priority.</p><h4>Design lesson</h4><p>Use the monitoring target that represents the application reachability you actually care about. Monitoring only the BGP peer detects peer failure but can miss failures beyond it. This is a clean example of control-plane health versus service-path health.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>get router info bgp summary
get router info routing-table details &lt;prefix&gt;
diagnose sys sdwan health-check
diagnose sys sdwan service
diagnose sys session list</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 5, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Fortinet</span><h3>6. OSPF tags redistributed into BGP</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-wrong-route-tag-added-to-bgp-routes-redistributed-from-ospf-214352">https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-wrong-route-tag-added-to-bgp-routes-redistributed-from-ospf-214352</a></p><h4>Why tags matter</h4><p>Route tags are frequently used to preserve route provenance across redistribution boundaries. A tag can identify “originally OSPF,” “originally BGP,” or a particular domain so the return boundary can reject the route and prevent feedback loops. That makes the tag part of the correctness of the design, not decorative metadata.</p><h4>Documented FortiOS issue</h4><p>Fortinet documents affected releases where very high OSPF tag values can be represented incorrectly after redistribution into BGP. A policy that expects an exact tag can therefore stop matching after protocol conversion.</p><h4>Operational consequence</h4><p>If a mutated tag is used for loop prevention, the route can be re-advertised into its source domain or selected by the wrong policy. This can create intermittent preference changes or genuine loops depending on the surrounding topology and administrative-distance relationships.</p><h4>Verification</h4><p>Check the tag in the OSPF LSDB before redistribution, inspect the route-map that matches/sets metadata, then inspect the BGP route after redistribution. Do not assume the value survived simply because both protocols support a tag/community-like field.</p><h4>Prevention</h4><p>Use conservative tag ranges on affected code, include tag preservation in upgrade regression tests, and favor explicit deny-on-return policy. In multivendor designs, document numeric ranges that are known to survive each vendor’s conversion path.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>get router info ospf database
get router info bgp network &lt;prefix&gt;
show router route-map</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 6, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Palo Alto Networks</span><h3>7. Advanced Routing Engine filter attachment points</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/advanced-routing/create-filters-for-the-advanced-routing-engine">https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/ngfw/networking/advanced-routing/create-filters-for-the-advanced-routing-engine</a></p><h4>Policy toolkit</h4><p>PAN-OS Advanced Routing Engine provides access lists, prefix lists, redistribution route maps, BGP route maps, AS-path access lists, and community lists. The important point is not only what each object can match but where that object is referenced.</p><h4>Attachment-point semantics</h4><p>A prefix list may participate in route acceptance into the local RIB, redistribution between protocols, advertisement to a peer, conditional advertisement, aggregation, or attribute manipulation. The same prefix match therefore has different operational meaning depending on the stage.</p><h4>Sequential evaluation</h4><p>Palo Alto documents sequential evaluation for prefix lists and route-map entries. Once an entry matches, the permit/deny or set behavior applies according to that policy object. Order and scope are therefore part of the policy, not just the individual match terms.</p><h4>Troubleshooting workflow</h4><p>Ask where the route first disappears: source RIB, redistribution candidate set, destination protocol RIB, or neighbor advertisement. Inspect the filter only at that first failing stage. If the route never entered BGP, changing outbound BGP filtering cannot fix it; if it exists in BGP but is absent from one peer, redistribution is probably not the boundary.</p><h4>Cross-vendor translation</h4><p>When translating a Cisco/Fortinet/Arista route-map to PAN-OS, first record the business intent and pipeline stage. Only then map match/set constructs. Command-name similarity is not semantic equivalence.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>Advanced Routing Engine &gt; Routing Profiles &gt; Filters
Inspect local RIB
Inspect redistribution and BGP Filtering Profile</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 7, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">Palo Alto Networks</span><h3>8. OSPF migration exceptions</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/pan-os/u-v/routing-engine-migration-reference/routing-protocol-migration-exceptions/ospf">https://docs.paloaltonetworks.com/pan-os/u-v/routing-engine-migration-reference/routing-protocol-migration-exceptions/ospf</a></p><h4>Migration risk</h4><p>PAN-OS documents migration exceptions between legacy routing behavior and the Advanced Routing Engine. OSPF export policy in the legacy model can match dimensions such as path type, area, next hop, interface, prefix, an